    "content": "Title: Surrogacy for Lactating Mothers | Care & Qualifications\n\nContent: Surrogacy for Lactating Mothers Lactating women can make great candidates for becoming a surrogate because they are already experienced in pregnancy and childbirth. They may also have finished growing their own families, have enjoyed being pregnant and breastfeeding and are willing and ready to help another person experience the joys of parenthood. I’ve wanted to be a surrogate for seven years. When I first started looking into surrogacy I didn’t meet the surrogate requirements, since I didn’t have my own children yet. A close friend and two of my aunts are unable to have children of their own. This pushed me even more to see if I qualified once our own family was complete. Now, we are finally able to pursue our dream of helping another family have the ability to have a child or sibling for their child! Breanna Allen Caring for Lactating Mothers As part of ConceiveAbilities Surrogate Care and Compensation Package, we offer fourth trimester postpartum support after a surrogate completes her surrogacy journey, including lactation, breastfeeding and surrogacy, and pumping support. In addition, you can make up to $72,000 as a first-time surrogate with the ConceiveAbilities $10,000 limited time bonus. Because we know moms like you make great surrogates, we prioritize your compensation. Overlapping Qualifications Lactating Mothers Have That Make Them Great Surrogates 5 Basic requirements to be a surrogate: Have you given birth before? A history of at least one full-term, healthy and uncomplicated pregnancy is essential to ensuring your surrogacy journey is healthy and successful. How old are you? At the time you are matched with intended parents, you should be between 21 and 40 years of age. Are you a long-term US resident? The surrogacy journey is lengthy and is accomplished only by working with a US-based IVF clinic. A current or pending deployment may add some extra considerations to the timing of when you are a surrogate but, with our expertise, we will help you find a way forward to be a surrogate. Are you physically healthy? You must have an uncomplicated medical history, not be over- or under-weight, not currently smoke, and be free of certain chronic conditions. Are you emotionally healthy? Your surrogacy journey is demanding and requires a stable foundation in your life without undue stress. Become a Surrogate",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/surrogate-lactation/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: Surrogacy Explained: Your Complete Guide to the Journey\n\nContent: Surrogacy with Conceiveabilities At ConceiveAbilities, we believe becoming a surrogate is one of the most courageous things someone can do. Start Your Surrogacy JourneyMany on our team have been surrogates or became parents through a gestational surrogate, shaping our commitment as a trusted surrogacy agency. We’ve built a process, leveraging our many years of experience, carefully designed to protect, support, and celebrate you throughout your surrogacy journey. If you’re curious, ready, or somewhere in between – we are your people. How to Become a Surrogate Becoming a surrogate with ConceiveAbilities involves five clear steps, each fully supported by our team of surrogacy experts. 1.Apply and ScreeningComplete our simple application process and complete our screening process to confirm you’re ready to become a surrogate.2.Get MatchedOur experienced surrogacy agency matches you with compatible intended parents, ensuring your comfort and alignment throughout your journey.3.Prepare for IVFOur experts guide you through the medication protocols and preparations for embryo transfer and pregnancy that are provided by your intended parents’ clinic.4.Carry the PregnancyReceive ongoing emotional, physical, and logistical support throughout your pregnancy from our dedicated match management team.5.Delivery and BeyondCelebrate this incredible milestone, with continuing support and recognition throughout your “fourth trimester” for your generous act as a surrogate.Learn About Becoming a SurrogateWho Qualifies to Be a Surrogate?Be between 21–42 years oldHave had at least one healthy pregnancy and deliveryBe a citizen or permanent resident of the United StatesWhat else? Complete the application and schedule an introductory call with one of our experts. We’re thrilled to know you and learn about your interest in surrogacy and, in that introductory call, we’ll tackle any other topics that could impact your qualifications to be a surrogate.Defining the Surrogate Mother What is a surrogate? Gestational surrogates, often called “surrogate mothers,” carry pregnancies for intended parents but have no genetic link to the child. While some people use “surrogate mother” casually, others prefer “surrogate” or “surrogate pregnancy” to better reflect the surrogate’s role. We use varied terminology to educate clearly and respectfully about this important family-building process. Understanding SurrogacySurrogate Support Program Our groundbreaking All-In Surrogate Care and Compensation Program provides unmatched support at every stage of your surrogacy journey. With our proprietary Matching Matters™ process, highest surrogate pay, comprehensive legal and medical coverage, and dedicated emotional support, we ensure you’re fully protected and empowered from application through postpartum recovery—the way surrogacy should be. Explore Surrogate SupportHow Are Surrogates Matched? Surrogates are matched with intended parents through our Matching Matters™ process based on shared values, expectations, and compatibility. We have dedicated staff just to understand your needs and expectations of who you see as your perfect match. You’ll have the opportunity to review the profile of any intended parent to decide whether you would like to meet them. And then, you’ll meet potential intended parents and choose who you feel most comfortable with. With a 97% first-time match success rate, we ensure meaningful relationships and smooth journeys from the start. See How Matching WorksWhat Medications Do Surrogates Take? You will work with your intended parents’ fertility clinic who will direct and guide your medication protocol to prepare your body for a successful transfer of the embryo and healthy pregnancy. These medications could include: birth control pills to regulate your cycle, Lupron to suppress ovulation, Estrogen to thicken your uterine lining, Progesterone and Medrol to support implantation, low-dose aspirin to improve blood flow, and antibiotics to prevent infection. While this may seem daunting, ConceiveAbilities manages a team of experts, with the fertility clinic at the center of this stage of your surrogacy journey, to provide guidance throughout the entire process, ensuring you’re comfortable and supported each step of the way. Learn More About Surrogacy MedicationsHow Much Do Surrogates Get Paid? First-time surrogates earn up to $75,000 – well above the national average – as participants in ConceiveAbilities’ All-In Care and Compensation program. Our simple payment structure ensures you’re financially supported every step of the way, with clear, upfront payments and no hidden requirements. Calculate Your Surrogate PaySurrogacy Laws By State Most states support compensated gestational surrogacy, with the exception of Nebraska and Louisiana. We offer detailed guidance on the legal landscape in every state to help you understand your rights and navigate the process with confidence. Check Your States Surrogacy LawsSurrogacy Terms Surrogacy is a complex journey with legal, medical, and psychological terms that can feel overwhelming at first. Our surrogacy glossary breaks it all down into clear, easy-to-understand language so you can know everything you need to throughout your surrogacy process. View the Surrogacy GlossaryWhat Surrogates Have to Say Others have stood where you are now—curious, hopeful, and ready to help. Hear firsthand from surrogates who partnered with ConceiveAbilities to make parenthood possible for families across the country. Hear From SurrogatesBeing a surrogate mom has been an incredibly fulfilling journey, and I couldn’t have asked for a better team to work with than Conceiveabilities. If you’re considering surrogacy, I wholeheartedly recommend this agency for their professionalism, empathy, and dedication. - Kelly, Chicago SurrogateI am a repeat surrogate with Conceiveabilities. I love working with this agency. They do such a wonderful job with their matching and their mental health team is fantastic! They’ve held my hand every step of the way and made me feel like I had a voice in how my journey progressed. - Christina, Colorado SurrogateWhat an amazing experience my Conceiveabilities journey was! They made sure communication was always happening and the structure behind the support system really empowered me. Being able to relax knowing everything had been talked about ahead of time, even things I hadn’t considered, really was a blessing. - Kimmee, Minnesota SurrogateOther Surrogacy Programs Looking to learn more about the different paths to surrogacy? Explore additional programs designed to meet unique needs and circumstances for both surrogates and intended parents. Refer a Surrogate Know someone who would make an amazing surrogate? Refer her to ConceiveAbilities and earn rewards while helping another family grow. Make a ReferralSurrogacy Advocate Program Our Surrogacy Advocate Program connects you with experienced surrogates who can answer your questions and share real insights. Get the support and perspective you need from someone who’s been there. Be a Surrogacy AdvocateSurrogacy FAQs More Surrogacy FAQsHow long does the surrogacy process take?The surrogate process may take between 15 to 18 months on average; however, any individual situation could take more or less time.If I am currently breastfeeding, can I apply to be a surrogate?Yes, should you meet the basic requirements, we will work with you in completing our qualification process. To start the surrogacy process itself, you must have completely weaned and have had at least one menstrual cycle.Can I apply to be a surrogate if I have an IUD in place for my birth control?Yes, you can apply to be a surrogate if you have an IUD and, assuming you meet the basic requirements, we will work through the entire qualification process with you. To start the surrogacy process itself, you must have had your IUD removed and have had at least one menstrual cycle.Does receiving government assistance disqualify me?No. A surrogate must demonstrate financial stability in order to qualify for the surrogacy program, however, receiving government assistance is not on its own a disqualifying factor.Ready to Begin Your Surrogacy Journey? You’ve learned what it means to be a surrogate—now it’s your turn to make a difference. When you partner with ConceiveAbilities, you’re supported, celebrated, and empowered from day one. Join the women who are helping build families and changing lives in the most meaningful way. Start Your ApplicationAdditional Resources Explore more topics and personal perspectives to support you wherever you are in your surrogacy journey. Surrogacy for Lactating MothersLearn how surrogates who choose to pump can provide continued care after delivery — and receive additional compensation for their time and effort.Surrogacy for Military WivesMilitary wives are uniquely positioned to be amazing surrogates. Discover how surrogacy can offer flexibility, purpose, and financial empowerment.Meet the Men Behind SurrogacySurrogacy isn’t just a women’s story — hear from the men who support, encourage, and stand proudly behind the journey.Lactation for New MothersUnderstand how lactation plays a role for intended mothers, and explore your options for feeding and bonding after surrogacy.Surrogacy in the Teaching CommunityMany teachers find surrogacy to be a natural extension of their nurturing spirit. Learn why educators make incredible surrogates.Surrogacy in the Nursing CommunityNurses bring compassion, expertise, and strength to surrogacy. See how their caregiving backgrounds make them ideal candidates for this life-changing journey.",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: Surrogacy Advocate Program | ConceiveAbilities\n\nContent: Surrogacy Advocate Program Our Surrogate Advocates are here to provide authentic insights about surrogacy and working with ConceiveAbilities. We encourage you to reach out to them directly so they may assist you in learning more about surrogacy and if it’s right for you. Click to read each surrogate’s bio and learn how to best reach them. Scroll to the bottom of the page to learn more about the program guidelines. Find Out Your Base Compensation Including Wage Replenishment Use Our Calculator Learn About Our All-In Surrogate Care and Compensation Package Go All-InDo You Meet Basic Surrogate Requirements? Take Our SurveyMedical or Qualification Questions? Connect with our Surrogate Engagement TeamWe get it. There is a lot of information about surrogacy out there and it is easy to feel overwhelmed or not know which step to take next. Although surrogacy is becoming more common, we do hear from many applicants that they had never met or even spoken directly with a real surrogate prior to applying to our program. We understand that this lack of familiarity and supporting reference can make surrogacy feel more distant. Many women find that it is very helpful to connect with an experienced surrogate, someone who has been where you are now and can speak to the more human aspects of surrogacy. The women listed above are uncompensated volunteers who feel so passionately about surrogacy as a means of touching other’s lives that they have offered to speak with any potential surrogate applicants who may want to connect. ConceiveAbilities does not compensate them — they do this to give back. We invite you to click on each image to read the surrogate’s bio and learn how to best reach her. Advocates who have included their phone numbers are open to calls or texts. We encourage you to connect with these wonderful ladies and to use them as the valuable resources they are; unlike any other resource, experienced surrogates can help you determine whether surrogacy is right for you. We know you will enjoy speaking with them. Of course, our acceptance team remains available to you as well. Don’t hesitate to reach out regarding any questions about the medical, legal or financial aspects of the journey. Surrogate Advocate Program Guidelines Our volunteer surrogate advocates are encouraged to share freely, as they see fit, and to their own level of availability and comfort. We do ask that our advocates and those who reach out to them to abide by the below guidelines. Safety Advocates have been asked to avoid in person meetings with any parties unknown to the program, for safety reasons. Advocates have been asked to decline any offer to connect on social media prior to an inviting applicant becoming known to the program. Compensation Surrogate compensation is based on state, experience and other variables. Our Advocates are not the best resource for such information; many have specified that they regard their personal compensation as a private matter and would rather not discuss it. If you have questions about compensation please look to, our compensation page, our compensation calculator, or reach out to our acceptance team, via email or contact form. Courtesy Our Advocates are busy moms with full lives who have generously offered to volunteer their time to connect with applicants. They are eager to speak with you, but we ask that you be respectful of their time. Requirements and Medical Although several Advocates are medical professionals, we ask that you direct all medical and requirement-related questions to the ConceiveAbilities’ nursing and acceptance team. ConceiveAbilites would like to express its sincerest thanks to our incredible Advocates. These women represent the heart of our community, and we are honored to work with them as they continue their calling through advocacy.",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/advocate-program/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: The Faces of Surrogacy | ConceiveAbilities\n\nContent: Surrogate Mother Bios Two futures at a time Courageous and compassionate, our surrogates are different. They are strong. Committed. They believe that everyone who wants to become a parent should be able to. They may be someone like you. Learn more about these amazing women by exploring our surrogate mothers who build a future for someone else’s family while pursuing their own. Answer the callAnswer the callAnswer the callAnswer the callAnswer the call Ready? Start hereRelated Blog Posts My Surrogacy Journey: Kristina’s Surrogacy StoryApril 26th, 2012We're thrilled that Kristina shared her thoughts from her experience as a surrogate mother. Read on to see how the decision changed her life – and the life of the family she helped to build. Read MoreSix Reasons to Use a Surrogate MotherSeptember 12th, 2018There are many reasons why a couple might choose to take the route of having a baby through a surrogate mother- here are just seven of the most common... Read More",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/surrogates/surrogate-bios/"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: Surrogacy Screenings Explained | ConceiveAbilities\n\nContent: One of the ways in which ConceiveAbilities differentiates itself from our competitors is our longstanding and downright stubborn commitment to working with only the most qualified, most stable, healthiest, and – frankly – most awesome women on the planet. Over the past 20 plus years, our “proudly picky” attitude has helped shape our multifaceted screening process. And although the different phases of our screening may, at first, feel a little daunting as an applicant, they help us get to know one another and, together, make a decision about whether a surrogacy journey can be a healthy and successful one for you. Below we highlight the major phases of our one-of-a-kind screening process. If you are in the midst of your screening process already, please remember your Surrogate Engagement Coordinator is always there to assist you with any specific questions or concerns you might have. Phase #1: Initial Application The screening process begins by filling out ConceiveAbilities’ online, initial application. This is a secure form found on the ConceiveAbilities’ website that allows you to submit your initial application and ensure that you satisfy ConceiveAbilities’ basic qualifications. These basic qualifications include: Age between 21-39 years old BMI below 35 Must have given birth before Must be a citizen or permanent resident of the United States As you fill out this initial form, you’ll receive information about why we ask each question. And, for a more detailed explanation of each qualification, check out the qualifications to become a surrogate. Phase #2: Phone Interviews After your initial application has been approved, your Surrogate Engagement Coordinator will conduct a phone “interview” with you. This call is a way for us to learn more about you, gain a better understanding of your personality, and also discuss some of the more delicate aspects of the journey directly and share more information about the process. Phase #3: Medical Records, Pap and Clearance Letter Next, our team will work with you to obtain your medical history with particular attention to your previous pregnancy(ies) and birth(s). We reward your assistance with this process, as your assistance ensures we can move forward on your timeline and not be bogged down by administrative paperwork. Our team will guide you through each step. Our staff will also prompt you to provide your most recent pap results and request that you obtain a “clearance letter” from your current doctor. A “clearance letter” is simply a communication from your doctor stating that he or she feels that you are healthy enough to become pregnant. Phase #4: Background and Credit Check As the process moves along, we will run both a background and credit check on yourself and your spouse or live-in partner. We run these checks to learn a bit more about you and your family. We are always hoping these checks will confirm what you have already told us. We should say right here and now that having debt or a less-than-perfect credit score will NOT automatically disqualify you. We are not that picky. A lot of people have debt in this day and age. We just need to confirm that your financial life does not have undue stressors. Phase #5: Psychological Evaluation and Virtual Home Check Your psychological evaluation is an important step in the process to become a surrogate. Your evaluation will always be conducted by a licensed mental health professional who will take the time to understand your motivations, address some of the more challenging aspects of the journey and ultimately provide an assessment of both your and your partner’s mental fitness for this pending experience. The virtual home check will be conducted by a ConceiveAbilities professional at an agreed upon time. The objective of the visit is for us to confirm the safety and health of your environment.. Once the home check and psychological evaluation have been completed, you are nearly at the end of the screening process. It should not be long until you receive your final approval and are ready to be matched with your intended parents – the family you were meant to help! Less than two percent of applicants are accepted into our program. Passing our entire screening makes you a part of a very elite group of women. ConceiveAbilities surrogates are uniquely qualified (medically, psychologically, physiologically, and logistically) to help make dreams come true! Being a part of this community is something to be proud of and we know you will be welcomed and supported throughout your surrogacy journey. Are you a woman who enjoyed a healthy and successful pregnancy? Do you have friends or family who have suffered from infertility or need assistance from someone else to build their family? Have you ever considered the role you could play in helping someone else build their family – as a surrogate? Talk to us to learn more about the possibilities to help someone else’s dream come true.",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/about/blog/surrogacy-screenings-explained"
  },
  {
    "content": "Title: A Calling Unlike Any Other: Why Women Become Surrogates | ConceiveAbilities\n\nContent: A Calling Unlike Any Other Surrogates are superheroes because of their selflessness and sacrifice, but like most superheroes, they’re just ordinary people stepping up for a greater cause. Each woman is unique: we have had surrogates that are stay-at-home mothers, teachers, lawyers, 911 dispatchers, and even students. They are all united by their mutual desire to contribute to an experience greater than themselves. There are a number of reasons to become a surrogate, but we’ll break down the some of the main motivating factors for the surrogates we’ve accepted. Reasons Women Become Surrogates Helping parents start/grow their family – Giving life and helping others is often at the top of every surrogate’s list. The ability to create life and aid a family in need fosters an indescribable feeling to those who choose surrogacy. It is a perspective and experience unique to women, and difficult to put into words or share with those who have not experienced it first hand. High Financial Compensation – It’s no secret that surrogates get paid a decent sum for their role. Surrogates at ConceiveAbilities are compensated anywhere between $44,00 to $62,000, depending on the type of pregnancy and the surrogate’s experience. Some may see compensation for pregnancy a questionable practice, but it is less about financial motivation and more about protection and fairness. The surrogate must undergo a rigorous screening phase and adhere to restrictions in diet and physical activity. The compensation is a means of fairly paying the surrogate for her time and effort. Empathy for those Struggling with Infertility – Infertility is more common than you think. According to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), roughly 10% of women in the United States (6.1 million) aged 15-44 have trouble getting or staying pregnant. There are a number of reasons for why a woman might be infertile, and surrogacy remains one of the safest alternatives to a genetically-related pregnancy. Surrogates help others affected by infertility by carrying a baby for them. The Experience of Being Pregnant – Pregnancy can be overwhelming, but for some, pregnancy is an enjoyable and unique experience. When some surrogates reach the end of their journey, they exhibit a nostalgia for their pregnancy, from the first kick, to the delivery. This is why we often get repeat surrogates- they usually want to relive that feeling of being a protector and carrier for an infant life. Be Part of the Discussion – Even in 2017, surrogacy has a mixed reputation. People continue to stigmatize it with misconceptions and outdated facts. Choosing to become a surrogate is a proactive way of getting your voice heard, becoming a part of the conversation, and breaking stereotypes behind what a surrogate is or what surrogacy is about. Many of our own surrogates have become advocates for surrogacy and women’s rights, whether it’s through the topics of fair compensation and coverage, or overall public acceptance. A Different Kind of Extended Family – A surrogate’s relationship with her intended parents and surrobaby is remarkably different from that of her own biological parents. Some surrogates prefer not to be bothered and go at it alone, while others want to get to know the parents on a more intimate level. We consider both types in the screening and matching phases. SurroSisterhood Community – Becoming a surrogate means joining a strong and tightknit community of former gestational carriers. Once you join, you’re always part of it. So what are the benefits of joining the community? You’ll be invited to surrogate parties, picnics, and gatherings. You’ll have a group of women who can help you out as you go through your journey, to ask questions about, to lean on in times of distress, and to celebrate with when that time comes. Here are a few examples of previous surrogacy events. Signing Up to Become a Surrogate No matter what your reason may be for wanting to be a surrogate, it never hurts to find out a little more information. At ConceiveAbilities, we only select the best, most elite surrogates for our program. We don’t just make sure they pass the requirements and background, we want to be absolutely sure that our surrogates are both physically and mentally healthy. They should also exhibit the more abstract qualities, the values that drive a surrogate: determined, courageous, and responsible. Think you have what it takes to become a surrogate? Read our list of requirements here. Want to learn more about the journey from the surrogate’s own words? Read our post The Surrogate’s Point of View. Interested in what the SurroSisterhood is all about? Read our story on the culture and community. To find out more news and information on the surrogacy industry and starting your own surrogacy journey, be sure to follow our blog.",
    "source": "https://www.conceiveabilities.com/about/blog/a-calling-unlike-any-other-why-women-become-surrogates"
  }
]

